## The Specification Pattern in C# – a hands‑on guide

The **Specification** pattern lets you isolate business rules from your domain objects and compose them in a flexible, declarative way.  
Think of a specification as a *predicate* that can be combined, reused, and even translated into an `Expression` for EF or any other query provider.

Below you’ll find:

1.  The core idea and terminology
2.  A generic `ISpecification<T>` interface
3.  A set of base/specification classes (and, or, not, etc.)
4.  Optional helpers for LINQ/EF (`Expression<Func<T,bool>>`)
5.  A real‑world example (e.g. `Order` filtering)
6.  Tips & pitfalls

---

### 1. Core Idea

| Concept | What it represents | Where it lives |
|---------|--------------------|----------------|
| **Specification** | “Can this entity satisfy this rule?” | A class that can be queried (`IsSatisfiedBy`) |
| **Composite** | Combine several specifications | `And`, `Or`, `Not` classes |
| **Expression** | For use with LINQ/EF | `ToExpression()` |

**Why use it?**

- Keeps business rules out of your entities
- Lets you compose rules at runtime
- Enables reuse across services, repositories, or even across APIs
- Can be translated into SQL if you expose `Expression<Func<T,bool>>`

---

### 2. The Generic Specification Interface

```csharp
public interface ISpecification<T>
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Checks if the candidate satisfies the rule.
    /// </summary>
    bool IsSatisfiedBy(T candidate);

    /// <summary>
    /// Optional: provides a LINQ/EF Expression for the rule.
    /// </summary>
    Expression<Func<T, bool>> ToExpression();
}
```

*Implementation notes*:

- `IsSatisfiedBy` is the “runtime” predicate – perfect for in‑memory validation.
- `ToExpression` is optional but very handy when you want the same rule to run on the DB side.

---

### 3. Base & Composite Specifications

```csharp
// Abstract base (keeps ToExpression optional)
public abstract class Specification<T> : ISpecification<T>
{
    public abstract bool IsSatisfiedBy(T candidate);
    public virtual Expression<Func<T, bool>> ToExpression() => null;
}

// Simple wrapper around a Func
public class FuncSpecification<T> : Specification<T>
{
    private readonly Func<T, bool> _predicate;
    public FuncSpecification(Func<T, bool> predicate) => _predicate = predicate;

    public override bool IsSatisfiedBy(T candidate) => _predicate(candidate);
}

// And / Or / Not
public class AndSpecification<T> : Specification<T>
{
    private readonly ISpecification<T> _left, _right;
    public AndSpecification(ISpecification<T> left, ISpecification<T> right)
    {
        _left = left;
        _right = right;
    }

    public override bool IsSatisfiedBy(T candidate) =>
        _left.IsSatisfiedBy(candidate) && _right.IsSatisfiedBy(candidate);

    public override Expression<Func<T, bool>> ToExpression()
    {
        var leftExp = _left.ToExpression();
        var rightExp = _right.ToExpression();

        if (leftExp == null || rightExp == null) return null;

        var param = Expression.Parameter(typeof(T), "x");
        var body = Expression.AndAlso(
            Expression.Invoke(leftExp, param),
            Expression.Invoke(rightExp, param));

        return Expression.Lambda<Func<T, bool>>(body, param);
    }
}

public class OrSpecification<T> : Specification<T>
{
    private readonly ISpecification<T> _left, _right;
    public OrSpecification(ISpecification<T> left, ISpecification<T> right)
    {
        _left = left;
        _right = right;
    }

    public override bool IsSatisfiedBy(T candidate) =>
        _left.IsSatisfiedBy(candidate) || _right.IsSatisfiedBy(candidate);

    public override Expression<Func<T, bool>> ToExpression()
    {
        var leftExp = _left.ToExpression();
        var rightExp = _right.ToExpression();

        if (leftExp == null || rightExp == null) return null;

        var param = Expression.Parameter(typeof(T), "x");
        var body = Expression.OrElse(
            Expression.Invoke(leftExp, param),
            Expression.Invoke(rightExp, param));

        return Expression.Lambda<Func<T, bool>>(body, param);
    }
}

public class NotSpecification<T> : Specification<T>
{
    private readonly ISpecification<T> _inner;
    public NotSpecification(ISpecification<T> inner) => _inner = inner;

    public override bool IsSatisfiedBy(T candidate) =>
        !_inner.IsSatisfiedBy(candidate);

    public override Expression<Func<T, bool>> ToExpression()
    {
        var innerExp = _inner.ToExpression();
        if (innerExp == null) return null;

        var param = Expression.Parameter(typeof(T), "x");
        var body = Expression.Not(Expression.Invoke(innerExp, param));
        return Expression.Lambda<Func<T, bool>>(body, param);
    }
}
```

> **Tip** – Use *extension methods* for fluent composition:

```csharp
public static class SpecificationExtensions
{
    public static ISpecification<T> And<T>(this ISpecification<T> left, ISpecification<T> right) =>
        new AndSpecification<T>(left, right);

    public static ISpecification<T> Or<T>(this ISpecification<T> left, ISpecification<T> right) =>
        new OrSpecification<T>(left, right);

    public static ISpecification<T> Not<T>(this ISpecification<T> inner) =>
        new NotSpecification<T>(inner);
}
```

Now you can write:

```csharp
var spec = isAdultSpec.And(hasValidEmailSpec).Or(isVIPSpec);
```

---

### 4. Making Specifications DB‑aware

When you expose `ToExpression()`, you can use the spec directly in LINQ to Entities:

```csharp
var users = dbContext.Users
                     .Where(userSpec.ToExpression())
                     .ToList();
```

If you want to keep the DB‑side translation clean, you can implement specs using expression trees from the start:

```csharp
public class EmailContainsSpec : Specification<User>
{
    private readonly string _domain;
    public EmailContainsSpec(string domain) => _domain = domain;

    public override bool IsSatisfiedBy(User u) => u.Email.Contains(_domain);

    public override Expression<Func<User, bool>> ToExpression() =>
        u => u.Email.Contains(_domain);
}
```

