### 5. Real‑world Example – Order Filtering

```csharp
// Domain
public class Order
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public bool IsShipped { get; set; }
    public decimal Total { get; set; }
    public DateTime OrderDate { get; set; }
    public string CustomerName { get; set; }
}
```

#### Specifications

```csharp
public class IsShippedSpec : Specification<Order>
{
    public override bool IsSatisfiedBy(Order o) => o.IsShipped;
    public override Expression<Func<Order, bool>> ToExpression() => o => o.IsShipped;
}

public class TotalGreaterThanSpec : Specification<Order>
{
    private readonly decimal _min;
    public TotalGreaterThanSpec(decimal min) => _min = min;
    public override bool IsSatisfiedBy(Order o) => o.Total > _min;
    public override Expression<Func<Order, bool>> ToExpression() => o => o.Total > _min;
}

public class OrderDateAfterSpec : Specification<Order>
{
    private readonly DateTime _date;
    public OrderDateAfterSpec(DateTime date) => _date = date;
    public override bool IsSatisfiedBy(Order o) => o.OrderDate > _date;
    public override Expression<Func<Order, bool>> ToExpression() => o => o.OrderDate > _date;
}
```

#### Building a composite spec

```csharp
var recentHighValueUnshippedSpec =
    new OrderDateAfterSpec(DateTime.Today.AddMonths(-1))
        .And(new TotalGreaterThanSpec(500m))
        .And(new NotSpecification<Order>(new IsShippedSpec()));
```

#### Using it

```csharp
// In‑memory
var resultInMemory = orders.Where(o => recentHighValueUnshippedSpec.IsSatisfiedBy(o)).ToList();

// LINQ‑to‑Entities
var resultDb = dbContext.Orders
                .Where(recentHighValueUnshippedSpec.ToExpression())
                .ToList();
```

---

### 6. Tips & Pitfalls

| Tip | Why |
|-----|-----|
| Keep specs *pure* (no side‑effects) | They’re reusable and testable |
| Prefer expression‑based specs for EF | Avoid client‑side evaluation |
| Use extension methods for readability | Fluent, chainable syntax |
| Store simple `Func<T, bool>` specs in a cache if you reuse them | Avoid recreating the same lambda each time |
| When specs become many, consider a *Specification* base class that automatically builds expressions from an `Expression` field. | Less boilerplate |

**Pitfalls**

- **Expression tree complexity** – deep compositions can lead to large trees that are hard to debug. Use a `SpecificationDebugger` if needed.
- **Entity tracking** – `IsSatisfiedBy` will load the whole entity into memory if you use navigation properties. Use eager loading or separate specs that only touch scalar properties.
- **Null handling** – ensure your specs guard against `null` inputs, especially when used in EF (`u => u.Email != null && u.Email.Contains(...)`).

---

### 7. Quick Starter – A full minimal demo

```csharp
// 1. Create the base interfaces & classes (copy from above)

// 2. Domain model
public class Product
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public string Category { get; set; }
    public decimal Price { get; set; }
}

// 3. Concrete specs
public class CategorySpec : Specification<Product>
{
    private readonly string _cat;
    public CategorySpec(string cat) => _cat = cat;
    public override bool IsSatisfiedBy(Product p) => p.Category == _cat;
    public override Expression<Func<Product, bool>> ToExpression() => p => p.Category == _cat;
}

public class PriceLessThanSpec : Specification<Product>
{
    private readonly decimal _max;
    public PriceLessThanSpec(decimal max) => _max = max;
    public override bool IsSatisfiedBy(Product p) => p.Price < _max;
    public override Expression<Func<Product, bool>> ToExpression() => p => p.Price < _max;
}

// 4. Compose
var cheapElectronics = new CategorySpec("Electronics")
                         .And(new PriceLessThanSpec(100m));

// 5. Use
var products = GetAllProducts(); // e.g. from DB or in-memory list
var matched = products.Where(cheapElectronics.IsSatisfiedBy).ToList();
// or
// var matched = dbContext.Products.Where(cheapElectronics.ToExpression()).ToList();
```

---

## Bottom line

- **`ISpecification<T>`**: the contract.
- **`Specification<T>`**: abstract base.
- **`And`, `Or`, `Not`**: composition helpers.
- **`ToExpression()`**: bridge to EF/LINQ.
- **Fluent extensions**: `And()`, `Or()`, `Not()` for readability.

With these building blocks you can model any business rule, reuse it across services, and keep your codebase clean and maintainable. Happy spec‑crafting!
